course: medicine
lesson: 58
title: Posture and eyes for sporty kids
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Backs and necks love moving and hate staying stuck; no chair can hold you up if you slide down into it, so the best posture is the next one: get up, stretch and change position every half hour.
explain: Chloe watches highlights with her phone in her lap, head hanging: an adult head weighs about 5 kg, and a study reported that bent far forward it loads the neck like about 27 kg, «it's giving old lady» (it feels like).
explain: Eyes need guarding in sport: Isla from Aberdeen climbs out of the pool saying «My een are nippin'!» (Scots for my eyes are stinging), and goggles that fit plus a shower before swimming help far more than blaming the chlorine.
explain: A small fast ball like a squash ball can hit the eye itself, everyday glasses can snap and cut, snow throws sunlight back up into your eyes, and any change in sight after a hit means stop, tell an adult and go to A&E the same day.
fact: Sitting still for hours stiffens the back and neck; getting up and moving often helps more than any special chair.
fact: An adult head weighs about 5 kg; a study reported that bending it far forward over a phone loads the neck like about 27 kg; holding the phone up nearer eye level keeps the head upright.
fact: Sport strengthens the tummy and back muscles that hold you up, which helps balance long days of sitting.
fact: Red, stinging pool eyes are often caused by chloramines, made when chlorine mixes with sweat and wee in the water.
fact: A squash ball is about 40 mm across, small enough to fit into the eye socket, so protective eyewear is recommended for squash.
fact: A hard hit to the eye can cause damage inside even if the outside looks fine; blurred sight, flashes or pain after a hit need a doctor the same day.
fact: Everyday glasses can break and cut the face in sport; sports glasses are made of tough plastic such as polycarbonate and often have a strap.
fact: Genes play a part in short sight, but studies show children who spend more time outdoors are less likely to become short-sighted.
fact: Fresh snow reflects up to about 80% of the sun's UV light, even on cloudy days, and UV can burn the surface of the eye, called snow blindness.
fact: Rubbing an eye with sand in it can scratch the clear front of the eye; rinsing with clean water washes it out gently.
fact: Muscles cool down and stiffen while you sit, so subs need a warm-up before going on.
fact: «Lush» is Welsh slang for great, and «bruv» is London slang for mate.
exercise: Freddie slumps in his gaming chair for 3 hours: «it's built for posture, innit». What helps his back most? | Getting up and moving every half hour | Sliding further down the chair to relax his spine | Buying a more expensive chair | Sitting dead still all day
exercise: 3 hours of gaming with a stretch break every 30 minutes. How many breaks? | 6 | 3 | 9 | 30
exercise: Why does Chloe's neck ache after watching with her head hanging down? | Bent forward, the head loads the neck much more | Phones give off neck-ache rays | Only old people's necks ache | Highlights are too exciting
exercise: Where should Chloe hold her phone? | Up nearer eye level | Down flat on her lap | On the floor between her feet | Above her head on the bed
exercise: Owen sits 6 hours at school and 3 on screens. Why is rugby practice good for his back? | It strengthens the muscles that hold him up | It makes him sit even longer | Rugby only uses the arms and shoulders, not the back | It stops the back from growing
exercise: Isla's eyes sting after swimming. What's often the cause? | Chlorine mixed with sweat and wee | Pure clean chlorine on its own, nothing else | Cold water and nothing else | Too much salt in the air
exercise: How can Isla protect her eyes next time? | Goggles that fit and a shower first | Keep her eyes open wider under the water | Swim only on her back with her face up | Rub her eyes hard after every single length
exercise: Why should Aaron wear eyewear for squash? | A squash ball can hit the eye itself | A squash ball is too big to reach it | Eyebrows stop every ball | Goggles make the ball bounce more
exercise: Sophie's eye is blurry with flashes after a ball hit it. What next? | Stop playing and go to A&E | Play on until full time | Rub it hard until it clears | Wait a week to see
exercise: Why are Rahul's normal glasses risky for cricket? | They can snap and cut his face | They make the ball look bigger | They fog up only in winter | They are too light to wear
exercise: Jack: «Short sight is just from my parents, innit?» What's true? | Genes matter, but outdoor time lowers the chance | It's only from parents, nothing helps | It's caused only by sunlight | Screens cure short sight
exercise: Ben skis on a grey day without goggles and his eyes are gritty by evening. Why? | Snow reflected UV into his eyes | Cold air froze the front of his eyes | UV can't pass through clouds, so it was the wind | He blinked too little because of the cold
exercise: Ethan gets sand in his eye at the long-jump pit. What should he do? | Rinse with clean water, don't rub | Rub it hard with his fingers to get it out | Blow on it with his breath until it's gone | Keep jumping, the tears will wash it away
exercise: Grace comes off the bench cold and pulls her hamstring. What was missing? | A warm-up before going on | A longer sit on the bench | A bigger phone screen | Stretching her neck only
exercise: Which is true about the best posture? | Changing position often | Sitting up dead straight all day | Lying on the floor to watch | Never moving your neck
test: Freddie's mate: «Gaming chairs fix your back, bruv». What would a physio say? | The chair can't sit for you; move often | Gaming chairs cure all back pain if you buy a good one | Normal chairs are dangerous | Any chair is fine if it's black
test: What would happen if Chloe lifted her phone to eye level and looked up every few minutes? | Her neck would ache less | Her neck would get weaker | Her eyes would stop working | Nothing would change at all
test: Owen from Cardiff: «Lush, so rugby is good for my back?» What does «lush» mean there? | Great | Sore | Boring | Wet
test: Isla's mum: «Too much chlorine, I'll complain to the pool». What else could be going on? | Swimmers not showering before getting in | The water being too deep | The tiles being too blue | The lifeguard's whistle
test: Aaron: «Mate, my eyebrow will protect me». Why does his dad go quiet and buy goggles? | A small fast ball can get past the bone | Eyebrows always stop squash balls before they reach the eye | Goggles make you play faster | Squash rules ban eyebrows
test: Sophie: «I'm fine, just let me play, ref!» Her eye looks normal from outside. What's true? | The serious damage can be inside the eye | No blood means no injury | She can play with one eye shut | She just needs a cold drink
test: Rahul from Toronto: «They're fine, eh». What should he ask for? | Proper sports glasses with a strap | Thinner everyday glasses so they weigh less | No glasses at all on the pitch | Sunglasses with glass lenses
test: A cartoon kid wears no goggles skiing on a cloudy day: «no sun, no problem». Correct it. | UV gets through cloud and bounces off snow | Clouds block all UV light | Snow soaks up all the light | Cold air protects the eyes
test: Jack's optician says «get outside more». What would happen if Jack played outside 2 hours a day? | His chance of short sight would likely drop | His eyes would definitely get worse | His glasses would vanish | Nothing could change at all
test: Grace: «Subs don't need to warm up, we're fresh». Which is true? | Sitting cools muscles, so subs warm up too | Sitting keeps muscles warmer | Only starting players need to warm up before kick-off | Fresh legs can't get pulled
test: Ethan's friend says «just rub it out, mate». What's the risk? | Scratching the front of the eye | Making the sand go away faster and safer | Making him jump further in the next round | No risk at all, rubbing is what tears are for
exam: Identify the principal means by which prolonged sitting is best counteracted. | Frequent movement and changes of position | The acquisition of a specialised and costly gaming chair | Remaining entirely motionless | Reclining further into the seat
exam: It is given that a participant's vision is blurred following an impact to the eye. State the appropriate course of action. | Cessation of play and same-day medical assessment | Continuation of play until the final whistle | Vigorous rubbing of the affected eye | Postponement of assessment for one week
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hazard presented by snow to the eyes? | Snow reflects ultraviolet light even under cloud | Snow absorbs all ultraviolet light | Snow presents no hazard whatsoever on overcast days | Snow reduces the need for eye protection
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which protective eyewear is recommended in squash. | The ball is small enough to enter the eye socket | The ball is too large to cause injury | The ball travels very slowly | The rules prohibit spectacles of any kind
